      },
      "lineNbr": 214,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-29T09:40:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Or maybe just check pkvm_cpu_initialized? If not initialized yet, then we just directly write the value to these MSRs?\n\nSounds like a good idea, but: if the current CPU is not initialized yet, it doesn\u0027t mean that the \"first\" CPU (which does \"global initialize\" of pKVM) is not initialized yet. So if the first CPU has already global-initialized pKVM, it has already set up the `kvm_uret_msrs_list`, so we _must_ do `kvm_set_user_return_msr` here rather than just directly write these values?\n\nMaybe export a function like `pkvm_global_initialized()` to check it here?\n\n...Or if we don\u0027t have a goal to make this truly robust (i.e. to allow the host to write these MSRs at any moment, even in parallel with pKVM initialization), - then maybe just add `BUG_ON(!pkvm_cpu_initialized())` here, for extra safety?\n\n\u003e BTW, as this can happen before sending enable_virtualization_cpu hypercall, the per_cpu user_return_msrs is not initialized yet, with all 0 values. But it should be fine for us to use kvm_set_user_return_msr here to write these MSRs.\n\nHmm yeah, another good point to think about...",
      "parentUuid": "e0156d38_545e9b9c",
      "revId": "a33471f42130f67e0c257bf91646f854a31aec54",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf59ef70_74b9400d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/vmx/host_vmx.c",
