      "message": "\u003e vmx_setup_user_return_msrs calls kvm_add_user_return_msr for MSR_EFER unconditionally and therefore kvm_probe_user_return_msr (which triggers an unconditional wrmsrq, not dependent on VM_ENTRY_LOAD_IA32_EFER, VM_EXIT_SAVE_IA32_EFER and VM_EXIT_LOAD_IA32_EFER wrmsrq). Also kvm_add_user_return_msr adds it to kvm_uret_msrs_list. So this write seem legit.\n\nWhat I meant is - even if wrmsrq writes the new value, the VMENTRY will overrite it with GUEST_IA32_EFER. So a genuine host request is discarded. \n\nConsider this sequence:\n```\nVMEXIT(intercept for MSR_EFER)\n  saves A into GUEST_IA32_EFER\n  loads pKVM EFER\n  runs Handler\n\nHandler:\n  wrmsrq writes B into physical pKVM EFER\n  sets cache.curr \u003d B\n  (GUEST_IA32_EFER is not updated and is the old value A)\n\nVMENTRY\n  reloads GUEST_IA32_EFER (which is still A)\n  host runs with A while it expects B\n```\n\nIs this possible? From reading code, I think the handler doesn\u0027t update `GUEST_IA32_EFER` but I might be wrong\n\nI think linux doesn\u0027t update  MSR_EFER after deprivilege, so should not be an issue. But still, it would be an expectation mismatch if ever host tries to update",
